{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates the variance of the lotteries to determine preference. Variance measures the spread of outcomes around the expected value and reflects risk. For Lottery A: outcomes are 6000 (25%) and 0 (75%). For Lottery B: outcomes are 4000 (25%), 2000 (25%), and 0 (50%). We compute the variance for each lottery. Variance = Σ [P(outcome) × (outcome - expected value)²]. First, compute expected values: EA = 0.25×6000 + 0.75×0 = 1500. EB = 0.25×4000 + 0.25×2000 + 0.5×0 = 1000 + 500 + 0 = 1500. Both have the same expected value (1500). Now compute variance: For A: (0.25×(6000−1500)² + 0.75×(0−1500)²) = 0.25×(4500)² + 0.75×(1500)² = 0.25×20,250,000 + 0.75×2,250,000 = 5,062,500 + 1,687,500 = 6,750,000. For B: (0.25×(4000−1500)² + 0.25×(2000−1500)² + 0.5×(0−1500)²) = 0.25×(2500)² + 0.25×(500)² + 0.5×(1500)² = 0.25×6,250,000 + 0.25×250,000 + 0.5×2,250,000 = 1,562,500 + 62,500 + 1,125,000 = 2,749,999.99 ≈ 2,750,000. Thus, Lottery A has a higher variance (6,750,000 > 2,750,000). Therefore, the decision reason prefers Lottery A due to higher variance. The information relevant to the decision reason (variance comparison) is fully derivable from the lotteries’ outcomes and probabilities.",
